 have the more  miserable we become so we think we're  excited about someone and then we swipe  and there's another amazing person who  appears we make a mistake which is that  too many people think of the faces on  their screen as options the truth is  you're not an option until you show  for the date and express interest in  someone basically most of the time  you're just a bubble on a screen the  executive producer producer of CBS n  originals that's Adam Yamaguchi joins us  now I love everything that they said  right there why is it why is it making  us more lonely to have all these options  and choices aside from the paradox of  choice or the paralysis of choice  paralysis that many people face is is  this phenomena of deindividuation and  and and the experts sort of alluded to  it on game D individual so when you're  on your phone you are shielded by your  screen your avatar is your proxy and  there's a veil of anonymity so people  are likely to engage in behaviors that  they might not in real life yeah and we  see this across social media this isn't  just a dating apps phenomenon and so  over time you know you begin to treat  other people not as people but as  objects or as options it becomes almost  like a video game for people absolutely  and and and before you know what you're  mindlessly treating it like a video game  and you're swiping swiping away and  what's interesting is that a lot of  these these apps have they've been built  from the ground up to feel like a game  color schemes the graphics the sounds  that come in when someone shows an  interest in you is gratifying you were  talking earlier about Instagram and the  sort of gratification that comes with  likes what's very similar on dating apps  so how do you make it positive Adam well  I think one of the things is you know  people so people can burn out but  experts say look you're on a dating app  for a reason you know you've lost sight  of what your goal is but if your goal is  to meet somebody